WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
Mendel crossed tall pea plants with short ones,

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.720
and every one of their offspring grew tall.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.480
The short trait seemed to have vanished.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.080
Then he let those tall offspring pollinate themselves.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.640
Out of 1,064 plants in the next

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.720
generation, 277 were short.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.120
So how can two tall plants have a

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.560
short offspring?

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.240
And how can a family tree tell us

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.160
the same kind of thing about people?

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:31.366
Remember, genes code for proteins, and many proteins

00:00:31.447 --> 00:00:32.686
are enzymes.

00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:35.206
The tall allele codes for an enzyme that

00:00:35.287 --> 00:00:36.486
makes a growth hormone.

00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:39.606
The short allele's version barely works.

00:00:39.927 --> 00:00:41.766
A plant with one of each still makes

00:00:41.847 --> 00:00:43.846
enough hormone, so it grows tall.

00:00:44.407 --> 00:00:47.606
That's what dominant means: the allele shows even

00:00:47.687 --> 00:00:48.966
with only one copy.

00:00:49.607 --> 00:00:52.646
The short allele is recessive: it shows only

00:00:52.727 --> 00:00:53.846
with two copies.

00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:58.186
Two of the same allele is called homozygous,

00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:00.426
and one of each is heterozygous.

00:01:01.147 --> 00:01:03.386
So which alleles go into a gamete?

00:01:03.947 --> 00:01:06.746
In meiosis, the two chromosomes of each pair

00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:10.026
separate, so each gamete gets just one allele

00:01:10.107 --> 00:01:11.266
of each gene.

00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:14.746
A heterozygous plant makes two kinds of gamete:

00:01:14.827 --> 00:01:17.226
half carry the tall allele, and half carry

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:17.946
the short one.

00:01:18.507 --> 00:01:20.586
That explains Mendel's first cross.

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:23.786
A tall plant with two tall alleles, crossed

00:01:23.827 --> 00:01:26.266
with a short plant, gives offspring that all

00:01:26.347 --> 00:01:27.546
get one of each.

00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:30.266
They're all tall, but every one carries the

00:01:30.347 --> 00:01:31.226
short allele.

00:01:32.434 --> 00:01:34.673
A Punnett square lays out every way the

00:01:34.754 --> 00:01:35.913
gametes can meet.

00:01:36.194 --> 00:01:38.593
One parent's gametes go across the top, the

00:01:38.754 --> 00:01:40.273
other's down the side.

00:01:40.594 --> 00:01:43.953
Each box is one equally likely fertilisation.

00:01:44.194 --> 00:01:46.673
Fill them in: one box with two tall

00:01:46.754 --> 00:01:50.433
alleles, two heterozygous boxes, and one with two

00:01:50.594 --> 00:01:51.673
short alleles.

00:01:51.954 --> 00:01:54.593
That's a genotype ratio of 1 to 2

00:01:54.754 --> 00:01:55.313
to 1.

00:01:55.954 --> 00:01:57.633
Three of the four boxes have a tall

00:01:57.834 --> 00:02:00.753
allele, so the phenotype ratio is 3 tall

00:02:00.994 --> 00:02:01.953
to 1 short.

00:02:02.514 --> 00:02:07.233
Mendel's 787 to 277 is very close.

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:10.353
But 3 to 1 is a chance for

00:02:10.434 --> 00:02:12.913
each offspring, not a promise for every group

00:02:12.994 --> 00:02:14.033
of four.

00:02:14.114 --> 00:02:15.953
Each seed has a one in four chance

00:02:16.034 --> 00:02:18.273
of being short, whatever happened to the seed

00:02:18.434 --> 00:02:19.313
before it.

00:02:19.474 --> 00:02:21.633
Plant just four seeds from this cross, and

00:02:21.714 --> 00:02:24.433
you'll get exactly one short plant only about

00:02:24.514 --> 00:02:26.593
42 per cent of the time.

00:02:26.714 --> 00:02:29.153
With hundreds of seeds, like Mendel had, the

00:02:29.314 --> 00:02:31.153
counts settle close to the ratio.

00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:34.126
So how can you tell whether a tall

00:02:34.207 --> 00:02:37.246
plant is homozygous or heterozygous, when they look

00:02:37.327 --> 00:02:37.886
the same?

00:02:38.447 --> 00:02:40.446
Cross it with a short plant, which can

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:42.046
only give the short allele.

00:02:42.527 --> 00:02:44.246
This is a test cross.

00:02:44.527 --> 00:02:47.486
A heterozygous tall plant gives each offspring a

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.486
half chance of being short.

00:02:49.607 --> 00:02:52.046
So if even one offspring is short, the

00:02:52.127 --> 00:02:54.726
tall parent must carry the short allele.

00:02:55.007 --> 00:02:58.046
If they're all tall, it's probably homozygous, and

00:02:58.127 --> 00:03:00.286
more offspring make that more certain.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.980
We can't run test crosses on people, so

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:07.660
instead we read family trees called pedigrees.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.940
Squares are males, circles are females, and a

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.820
shaded symbol means the person has the trait.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.460
A line joins two parents, and a line

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.300
down from it leads to their children.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.260
Each generation gets a Roman numeral, and each

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.580
person a number, so you can point to

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:24.940
exactly who you mean.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.260
In this video, the genes are all on

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.100
autosomes, the chromosomes that aren't sex chromosomes.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:34.860
Here's a family with a recessive condition, such

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:36.300
as cystic fibrosis.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.820
In generation one, an affected father has two

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.180
unaffected children.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.060
Every child of his got one recessive allele

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.100
from him, so his daughter carries it, hidden.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:52.060
In generation three, it reappears: two unaffected parents

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:53.500
have an affected daughter.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:54.940
That's the giveaway.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.180
If the parents don't show it, but their

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:00.620
child does, each parent must carry one hidden

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:01.660
recessive allele.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.460
People like this are called carriers.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:08.060
So recessive traits can skip a generation.

00:04:09.267 --> 00:04:12.626
Now a dominant condition, such as Huntington's disease.

00:04:13.107 --> 00:04:15.906
Look for clues: it usually shows up in

00:04:15.987 --> 00:04:19.106
every generation, and every affected child has an

00:04:19.307 --> 00:04:20.386
affected parent.

00:04:20.707 --> 00:04:24.226
An affected, heterozygous parent and an unaffected parent

00:04:24.547 --> 00:04:27.266
give each child a half chance, the same

00:04:27.347 --> 00:04:29.266
square as the test cross.

00:04:29.307 --> 00:04:32.386
The giveaway here is the opposite: two affected

00:04:32.467 --> 00:04:35.186
parents with an unaffected child.

00:04:35.307 --> 00:04:38.466
Each parent must carry a hidden recessive allele,

00:04:38.547 --> 00:04:41.026
so the condition must be dominant.

00:04:41.107 --> 00:04:43.346
And dominant doesn't mean common.

00:04:43.454 --> 00:04:45.666
Huntington's is dominant, but rare.

00:04:46.867 --> 00:04:48.146
Here's how to remember them.

00:04:48.707 --> 00:04:50.946
Hidden in the parents: recessive.

00:04:51.427 --> 00:04:53.426
Hidden in the child: dominant.

00:04:53.987 --> 00:04:55.906
In an answer, name the people.

00:04:56.307 --> 00:04:59.666
For example: individuals 3 and 4 in generation

00:04:59.827 --> 00:05:03.426
two are unaffected, but their daughter, individual 1

00:05:03.507 --> 00:05:06.706
in generation three, is affected, so the condition

00:05:06.867 --> 00:05:07.666
is recessive.

00:05:08.147 --> 00:05:10.626
Then write their genotypes, and use a Punnett

00:05:10.707 --> 00:05:12.386
square for any probability.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.200
Two harder questions now.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.960
First, this pedigree shows a condition caused by

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.640
a gene on an autosome.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.680
Is the condition dominant or recessive?

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.040
Justify your answer using numbered individuals.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.800
Then give the genotypes of individuals 1 and

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.000
2 in generation one, and find the probability

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.600
that their next child is unaffected.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.240
Pause here to answer.

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:49.786
It's dominant.

00:05:50.027 --> 00:05:53.066
Individuals 1 and 2 in generation one are

00:05:53.187 --> 00:05:56.426
both affected, but their daughter, individual 1 in

00:05:56.507 --> 00:05:59.226
generation two, is unaffected.

00:05:59.347 --> 00:06:02.426
So each parent carries a hidden recessive allele:

00:06:02.627 --> 00:06:04.506
both are heterozygous.

00:06:04.627 --> 00:06:06.586
Their square gives a one in four chance

00:06:06.667 --> 00:06:09.146
that the next child is unaffected.

00:06:09.227 --> 00:06:11.546
If you wrote "dominant, because most of the

00:06:11.747 --> 00:06:14.506
family has it", that isn't evidence.

00:06:14.547 --> 00:06:15.626
Cite the people.

00:06:16.834 --> 00:06:17.313
Second.

00:06:17.394 --> 00:06:18.593
In peas,

00:06:19.194 --> 00:06:21.633
round seeds are dominant to wrinkled seeds.

00:06:22.194 --> 00:06:24.273
A plant grown from a round seed is

00:06:24.354 --> 00:06:26.193
crossed with a plant grown from a wrinkled

00:06:26.274 --> 00:06:26.673
seed.

00:06:27.234 --> 00:06:30.833
The cross produces 52 round seeds and 48

00:06:30.994 --> 00:06:31.953
wrinkled seeds.

00:06:32.674 --> 00:06:34.353
Use a Punnett square to work out the

00:06:34.434 --> 00:06:36.273
genotype of the round parent.

00:06:36.754 --> 00:06:39.793
Then explain why the counts aren't exactly 50

00:06:39.874 --> 00:06:40.433
to 50.

00:06:40.994 --> 00:06:42.273
Pause here to answer.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:55.287
The wrinkled parent can only give the wrinkled

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:55.900
allele.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.020
Some offspring are wrinkled, so the round parent

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.620
gave a wrinkled allele too.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.500
It's heterozygous.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:05.500
The square gives half round, half wrinkled.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:08.540
Each seed is a separate chance, so 100

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:11.340
seeds vary a little around 1 to 1.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.620
If you said the round parent is homozygous,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:17.100
that cross would give only round seeds.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.820
So for any cross: write the parents' genotypes,

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:24.940
split each into its gametes, fill the square,

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.340
and read the ratios as chances for each

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:28.460
offspring.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.260
For a pedigree, look for the giveaway, test

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.500
it against named people, and then use the

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:34.460
square.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:38.220
Next: genes on the X chromosome, and alleles

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.620
that aren't simply dominant or recessive.

00:07:41.834 --> 00:07:43.273
Here are the key notes.

00:07:43.514 --> 00:07:45.433
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:45.514 --> 00:07:46.313
to copy them.
